---
import BaseHead from '../../components/BaseHead.astro';
import ProjectHeader from '../../components/ProjectHeader.astro';
import ProjectFooter from '../../components/ProjectFooter.astro';
const description = 'Puzzle design and gameplay systems for Odysseia, a myth-inspired 3D adventure by Gaucho Game Lab.';
const steps = [ ['Emission', 'Connect a source to a receiver.'], ['Redirection', 'Build a path through relay nodes.'], ['Dispersion', 'Split white light into RGB channels.'], ['Fusion', 'Recombine channels into new colors.'], ['Filtering', 'Pass only the required channels.'] ];
---
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head><BaseHead title="Odysseia" description={description} /></head>
<body>
<a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>
<ProjectHeader />
<main id="main">
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <p class="kicker"><span>GAME DEVELOPMENT / 2024–25</span><span>GAUCHO GAME LAB</span></p>
    <h1>Odysseia<span>A world of connected puzzles.</span></h1>
    <div class="hero-copy"><p>A myth-inspired 3D adventure. As <strong>Puzzle Lead</strong>, I turned tides, time, and light into reusable mechanics and levels in Unity.</p><a class="button" href="#tides">Explore the systems <span>↓</span></a></div>
    <figure class="cover"><img src="/projects/odysseia/cover.webp" alt="Odysseia title artwork showing a ship sailing through a mountainous coastal landscape" width="1672" height="941" fetchpriority="high" /><figcaption>ODYSSEIA / A MYTH-INSPIRED ADVENTURE · GAUCHO GAME LAB</figcaption></figure>
    <dl class="metadata"><div><dt>ROLE</dt><dd>Puzzle Lead</dd></div><div><dt>TEAM</dt><dd>15–20 developers</dd></div><div><dt>DURATION</dt><dd>Sep 2024 – Mar 2025</dd></div><div><dt>TOOLS</dt><dd>Unity · C#</dd></div></dl>
  </section>
  <nav class="chapter-nav" aria-label="Project sections"><div class="wrap"><a href="#overview"><span>00</span>Contribution</a><a href="#tides"><span>01</span>Tides &amp; time</a><a href="#light"><span>02</span>Light puzzles</a><a href="#labyrinth"><span>03</span>Labyrinth</a><a href="#implementation"><span>04</span>Implementation</a></div></nav>
  <section id="overview" class="contribution wrap">
    <div class="contribution-intro">
      <header class="chapter-heading"><p class="eyebrow">00 / OVERVIEW</p><h2>Overview</h2></header>
      <p>My work on <em>Odysseia</em> focused on designing reusable puzzle systems that could grow in complexity while remaining readable to the player.</p>
      <p>I developed the mechanics, implemented them in Unity, designed levels around their interactions, and collaborated with the art team to improve visual communication and puzzle readability.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="contribution-panel">
      <h2>My Contributions</h2>
      <ul class="contribution-points">
        <li>Designed and implemented the <strong>dynamic tidal system</strong> and <strong>time acceleration mechanic</strong>.</li>
        <li>Built a modular <strong>light puzzle system</strong> supporting redirection, dispersion, color fusion, and filtering.</li>
        <li>Designed <strong>two tidal levels</strong> and multiple <strong>light puzzle levels</strong>.</li>
        <li>Developed the layout, puzzle dependencies, and traversal flow of the <strong>Minotaur’s Labyrinth</strong>.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="tides" class="chapter wrap">
    <header class="chapter-heading"><p class="eyebrow">01 / TIDES &amp; TIME</p><h2>Tides &amp; Time</h2><p class="chapter-subtitle">The right route. At the right moment.</p><p>Water rises and falls with the day-night cycle. A route is no longer just a place to discover; it is a condition to create.</p></header>
    <div class="image-pair"><figure><img src="/projects/odysseia/tide-high.webp" alt="High tide in the coastal puzzle environment" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>FIG. 01 / HIGH TIDE — WATER COVERS OBSTACLES</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="/projects/odysseia/tide-low.webp" alt="Low tide exposing terrain and passages" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>FIG. 02 / LOW TIDE — TERRAIN BECOMES ACCESSIBLE</figcaption></figure></div>
    <div class="notes"><article><span class="eyebrow">THE RULE</span><h3>Different obstacles, different tides</h3><p>Low water lets a ship’s mast pass beneath a low-clearance channel. High water lets the ship cross rocks and reefs. Players must plan both where to go and when to act.</p></article><article><span class="eyebrow">THE ITERATION</span><h3>Make the state readable</h3><p>Early levels left players guessing whether the tide had peaked. We added a tidal indicator so they could judge the water level and decide when to move.</p></article></div>
    <div class="media-text"><figure><video autoplay muted loop playsinline controls preload="metadata" aria-label="Gameplay demonstration of time acceleration changing the tidal state"><source src="/projects/odysseia/time-speed.webm" type="video/webm" /></video><figcaption>FIG. 03 / TIME ACCELERATION IN ACTION</figcaption></figure><div><p class="eyebrow">PLAYER AGENCY</p><h3>Change time.<br />Don’t just wait for it.</h3><p>I implemented time acceleration so players could deliberately advance the day-night cycle. The environment becomes something to manipulate, turning passive waiting into an active puzzle decision.</p></div></div>
  </section>
  <section id="light" class="dark-section"><div class="chapter wrap">
    <header class="chapter-heading"><p class="eyebrow">02 / THE LIGHT SYSTEM</p><h2>Modular Light Puzzle System</h2><p class="chapter-subtitle">One simple rule. Many ways to combine it.</p><p>Start with a beam and a receiver. Introduce one new operation at a time, then build depth through combinations of familiar rules.</p></header>
    <ol class="progression">{steps.map(([name,text],i) => <li>
      <span class="eyebrow">0{i+1} →</span>
      <svg class="rule-diagram" viewBox="0 0 180 96" role="img" aria-label={[
        'A white beam connects an emitter to a receiver.',
        'A relay turns a beam toward another receiver.',
        'A prism splits white light into red, green, and blue beams.',
        'Red and green beams combine into a yellow beam.',
        'A green filter passes green light and blocks red and blue.'
      ][i]}>
        {i === 0 && <g><circle cx="18" cy="48" r="9" fill="#f2f2de" /><path d="M29 48H148" stroke="#f2f2de" stroke-width="4" /><rect x="150" y="37" width="22" height="22" rx="2" fill="none" stroke="#b8ca91" stroke-width="2" /></g>}
        {i === 1 && <g><circle cx="18" cy="72" r="7" fill="#f2f2de" /><path d="M27 72H90V23H149" fill="none" stroke="#f2f2de" stroke-width="4" /><circle cx="90" cy="72" r="7" fill="#22271f" stroke="#b8ca91" stroke-width="2" /><rect x="150" y="13" width="21" height="21" fill="none" stroke="#b8ca91" stroke-width="2" /></g>}
        {i === 2 && <g><path d="M8 48H70" stroke="#f2f2de" stroke-width="4" /><path d="M72 48L166 15" stroke="#ee8d82" stroke-width="3" /><path d="M72 48H166" stroke="#9fc685" stroke-width="3" /><path d="M72 48L166 81" stroke="#82b9e7" stroke-width="3" /><path d="M74 29L92 63H56Z" fill="#22271f" stroke="#d7ddca" stroke-width="2" /><text x="171" y="19">R</text><text x="171" y="52">G</text><text x="171" y="85">B</text></g>}
        {i === 3 && <g><path d="M8 17L86 48" stroke="#ee8d82" stroke-width="3" /><path d="M8 79L86 48" stroke="#9fc685" stroke-width="3" /><path d="M90 48H167" stroke="#ffe000" stroke-width="5" /><circle cx="87" cy="48" r="9" fill="#22271f" stroke="#d7ddca" stroke-width="2" /><text x="9" y="11">R</text><text x="9" y="94">G</text><text x="139" y="34">R+G</text></g>}
        {i === 4 && <g><path d="M8 24H79" stroke="#774e49" stroke-width="3" /><path d="M8 48H168" stroke="#a0ed76" stroke-width="5" /><path d="M8 72H79" stroke="#456379" stroke-width="3" /><rect x="80" y="12" width="20" height="72" fill="#9fc685" fill-opacity=".2" stroke="#9fc685" stroke-width="2" /><path d="M112 20L120 28M120 20L112 28M112 68L120 76M120 68L112 76" stroke="#59614e" stroke-width="2" /><text x="154" y="35">G</text></g>}
      </svg><h3>{name}</h3><p>{text}</p>
    </li>)}</ol>
    <p class="system-reasoning">Redirection turns a simple source-to-receiver connection into a routing problem. Dispersion creates branching paths, while fusion and filtering introduce dependencies between them, allowing more complex puzzles to emerge from familiar rules.</p>
    <div class="image-pair"><figure><img src="/projects/odysseia/light-filter.webp" alt="Colored surface filtering a beam in a light puzzle" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>FIG. 04 / FILTERING — SELECT WHICH CHANNELS PASS</figcaption></figure><figure><img src="/projects/odysseia/light-complex.webp" alt="Multi-room light puzzle with beams and receivers" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>FIG. 05 / COMPOSITION — CONNECT RULES ACROSS ROOMS</figcaption></figure></div>
  </div></section>
  <section id="labyrinth" class="chapter wrap">
    <header class="chapter-heading"><p class="eyebrow">03 / MINOTAUR’S LABYRINTH</p><h2>Minotaur’s Labyrinth</h2><p class="chapter-subtitle">A mechanic becomes the map.</p><p>I wanted players to explore the labyrinth without either skipping most of it or following one long, prescribed route. I used three interconnected color regions to balance local puzzle solving with dependencies across the larger space.</p></header>
    <figure class="concept">
      <svg class="annotated-maze" viewBox="0 60 1140 1060" role="img" aria-labelledby="maze-title maze-desc">
        <title id="maze-title">Minotaur’s Labyrinth: RGB routes and cross-region dependencies</title>
        <desc id="maze-desc">An enlarged crop of the original design sketch. Red routes occupy the left region, green routes the lower middle, and blue routes the right. A green beam enters the red region near the upper left and combines into yellow. The Minotaur is drawn in the central chamber.</desc>
        <svg x="40" y="80" width="1060" height="1010" viewBox="20 25 1040 1000"><image href="/projects/odysseia/labyrinth-concept.webp" width="1925" height="1090" /></svg>
        <g class="map-pin"><circle cx="180" cy="630" r="22" fill="#943d36" /><text x="180" y="638">A</text><circle cx="660" cy="855" r="22" fill="#39715a" /><text x="660" y="863">B</text><circle cx="980" cy="620" r="22" fill="#336b99" /><text x="980" y="628">C</text><circle cx="330" cy="130" r="24" fill="#866622" /><text x="330" y="138">D</text><circle cx="610" cy="535" r="24" fill="#866622" /><text x="610" y="543">E</text></g>
      </svg>
      <figcaption>
        <span>FIG. 06 / LABYRINTH DESIGN SKETCH — COLOR ROUTES &amp; CROSS-REGION DEPENDENCIES</span>
        <div class="maze-legend" aria-label="Labyrinth diagram legend">
          <ul class="region-key"><li><span class="legend-letter red">A</span>Red region</li><li><span class="legend-letter green">B</span>Green region</li><li><span class="legend-letter blue">C</span>Blue region</li></ul>
          <div class="maze-key"><div><h3><span class="legend-letter accent">D</span>Cross-region dependency</h3><p>Green light enters the red region and combines with red to produce yellow, unlocking a route.</p></div><div><h3><span class="legend-letter accent">E</span>Central goal</h3><p>The Minotaur’s chamber anchors the maze’s connected regions.</p></div></div>
        </div>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <a class="text-link" href="/projects/odysseia/labyrinth-concept.webp" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open original sketch &amp; handwritten notes ↗</a>
    <div class="labyrinth-explanation"><p>Each region offers focused puzzles without forcing one long, linear solution.</p><p>Solved beams remain visible, recording progress in the environment and suggesting routes still to explore.</p></div>
    <div class="prototype-group">
    <figure class="prototype"><img src="/projects/odysseia/labyrinth-prototype.webp" alt="Playable labyrinth graybox showing corridors, gates, and light puzzle nodes" width="2936" height="1578" loading="lazy" /><figcaption>FIG. 07 / PLAYABLE GRAYBOX PROTOTYPE</figcaption></figure>
    <p class="media-note">I built this playable graybox to explore the labyrinth’s layout, light-node placement, and puzzle dependencies. It let teammates experience the intended traversal and puzzle flow before the level’s final implementation.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section id="implementation" class="implementation"><div class="chapter wrap">
    <header class="chapter-heading"><p class="eyebrow">04 / UNDER THE HOOD</p><h2>Light System Architecture</h2><p class="chapter-subtitle">Propagate light. Decouple the reaction.</p><p>A modular, event-driven C# architecture keeps the light system independent from the gameplay objects it activates.</p></header>
    <figure class="component-architecture">
      <a href="/projects/odysseia/light-architecture.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener" aria-label="Enlarge light system component references and event flow diagram">
        <img src="/projects/odysseia/light-architecture.svg" width="1600" height="840" loading="lazy" alt="Component references and event flow: LightReceiver invokes DoorEvent and LightEvent; LightEvent controls LightBeam, which activates another GameObject’s receiver. RotationTrigger separately controls relay rotation and prompt visibility." />
      </a>
      <figcaption>FIG. 08 / COMPONENT REFERENCES &amp; EVENT FLOW <a href="/projects/odysseia/light-architecture.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OPEN FULL SIZE ↗</a></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="notes"><article><h3>Events connect the systems</h3><p>Receivers emit activation and deactivation events. Independent components respond by opening doors, rotating objects, or activating secondary beams.</p></article><article><h3>Shared logic, specialized rules</h3><p>A shared <code>LightReceiver</code> handles dependencies, color aggregation, activation state, and downstream propagation. <code>FilterLightReceiver</code> and <code>SpecificLightReceiver</code> derive from <code>LightReceiver</code> and override its virtual methods to filter color channels and activate on a target-color match, respectively.</p></article></div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="chapter wrap collaboration">
    <div class="collaboration-content">
      <header class="chapter-heading"><p class="eyebrow">WORKING WITH THE TEAM</p><h2>Team Collaboration</h2><p class="chapter-subtitle">Build. Review. Play. Refine.</p></header>
      <div class="collaboration-details info-grid">
        <article class="info-card"><h3>Planning &amp; Scope</h3><p>Before implementation, I wrote proposals outlining the approach and files I planned to modify. I broke the work into steps and time estimates for approval by the Game Lead and Tech Lead, making scope and implementation decisions explicit before coding.</p></article>
        <article class="info-card"><h3>Review &amp; Integration</h3><p>My pull requests were reviewed by both leads, and I addressed their feedback before merging. Working in one-week Scrum sprints, we integrated completed work into the game and used the team’s discussion to set tasks for the next sprint.</p></article>
        <article class="info-card"><h3>Across Disciplines</h3><p>I shared the labyrinth’s playable graybox with teammates to gather feedback on layout, puzzle dependencies, and traversal flow before final implementation. I also worked with artists to improve mechanic readability and environmental cues, connecting gameplay logic, level design, and visual presentation.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="closing"><p class="eyebrow">WHAT THIS PROJECT TAUGHT ME</p><p>Designing reusable puzzle systems taught me to connect mechanics, level structure, and player understanding. Adding the tidal indicator showed how clear feedback helps players decide when to act. Team reviews and playable prototypes helped me communicate design decisions and refine them with others.</p></div></section>
</main>
<ProjectFooter />
<script>
  const preference = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)');
  const updateVideos = () => document.querySelectorAll('video').forEach(video => {
    if (preference.matches) { video.pause(); video.autoplay = false; }
    else { video.autoplay = true; video.play().catch(() => {}); }
  });
  updateVideos();
  preference.addEventListener('change', updateVideos);
</script>
</body>
</html>
